    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "27-29",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "A club that spent the weekend flirting with trouble finally showed some backbone last night - <strong>Kazuma Okamoto's tiebreaking homer in the sixth carried the Blue Jays past the Marlins 2-1</strong>, and that's the sort of thin, hard-earned win a team in need of a pulse will happily take. Toronto is now 27-29, still third in the AL East and 8.5 games back, but the more encouraging number is the W2 streak and the fact it has won eight of its last 12, a small climb out of the mud after back-to-back losses to Miami and Pittsburgh. Okamoto has been the headline again, and Jeff Hoffman and Tyler Rogers did the late work to preserve it, a sign the bullpen can still close a door when asked. The Blue Jays are still carrying the look of a club stuck between buyer and chaser, but at least the recent run has given the afternoon a little more purpose. <strong>Now comes the next test: a Thursday trip to Baltimore, where the Orioles await at 5:35 PM ET and Toronto has to prove the Marlins win was a turn, not a pause.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 28, 2026"
      },
